The  command  changes a user to a new primary group by starting a
new shell.  The user remains logged in and the current  directory
and  file  creation  mask  remain  unchanged.  The user is always
given a new shell even if the primary group  change  fails.   The
command accepts the following options: The environment is changed
to  what  would be expected if the user actually logged in again.
This simulates a full login.  The is a group name or non‐negative
numeric group ID from the group database.  The real and effective
group IDs are set to or the group ID associated  with  the  group
name.   If  is not specified, restores the user’s real and effec‐
tive group IDs to the user’s primary group specified in the pass‐
word database.  The user’s supplementary group IDs  are  restored
to  the set specified for the user in the group database.  If the
user is not a member of the specified group, and  the  group  re‐
quires  a password, the user will be prompted for the group pass‐
word.  The group database The user database A  Version  7  format
password  file  If  a new shell is started the exit status is the
